leaves the front matter. BART joins Suggests with this article.

## Introduction

A Dirichlet prior on the covariate-inclusion weights, s ~ Dirichlet(theta
/ p), with a tessellation's active subset drawn without replacement, so
the prior on a subset is the product of its weights over the elementary
symmetric polynomial as normaliser, and theta on a 1000-point grid
through lambda = theta / (theta + rho) with Beta(a, b) weights. Linero
(2018) on tessellations; the BART package ships it as `sparse = TRUE`
with the same `a`, `b` and `rho`. Reach for it when most covariates are
noise: the uniform prior spends its dimension budget on them.

Prerequisite: BART (Chipman, George and McCulloch 2010) and AddiVortes
(Stone and Gosling 2025).

## The model

The three display lines of `docs/models.md`, "DART inclusion"; defaults
a = 0.5, b = 1, rho = p. Link to `crates/thiessen/src/ensemble.rs`.

## Example

```{r example}
source("_setup.R")
data <- friedman(n = 300, p = 50, sd = 1, seed = 1)
control <- thiessen_control(tessellations = 50, general_params = schedule)
dart <- thiessen_control(
  tessellations = 50, general_params = schedule,
  mean_params = term_params(structure = structure_params(inclusion = dart_inclusion()))
)
fits <- paired_fits(data, list(default = control, dart = dart), seeds)
summary(fits$dart[[1]])
score(fits, data)
```

Comparator, one fit per seed: `BART::wbart(sparse = TRUE, ntree = 50,
nskip = 300, ndpost = 300)` at matched size, scored on the same
held-out rows through `score(comparator = )`, which arrives with this
article. Linero (2018) uses p up to 1000; the knit cap sets p = 50. The
comparison shows the sparsity mechanism transfers; it is a reference
point, not a race.

## What it shows

Figure 1, mechanism: sorted inclusion proportions from
`variable_inclusion()`, uniform against DART, the five true columns
marked; second panel, the sampled `inclusion_weight[j]` beside
`wbart`'s `varprob`, and `concentration` against its Beta(a, b) prior.

```{r mechanism}
```

Figure 2, comparison: paired RMSE per seed, default against DART, with
the `wbart(sparse = TRUE)` point beside; the table adds the number of
true columns in the top five proportions.

```{r comparison}
```

Figure 3, boundary: the same at p = 10; nothing is gained when there is
little to exclude, and the grid on theta costs a Metropolis step per
sweep.

```{r boundary}
```

## Validity

Stem `dart`. Mention the broken-sampler fixture that drops the
normalisers from the weight update.

```{r battery, results = "asis"}
cat(readLines("figures/dart/battery.md"), sep = "\n")
```

